{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788222477,"nonce":"YRH+K9fxuAQcQLaSljMCwVYl7xc=","title":"dev:starter-kit must land its gear forbidden, the way a real drop-pod arrival does","message":"Resolved by Evan, 2026-08-31 (session 10): **make it mimic the landing.**\n\n## Why this is not cosmetic\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/StarterKit.cs` contains NO REFERENCE TO FORBIDDING AT ALL —\na grep for `forbid`/`Forbid` over that file returns nothing. Everything\n`dev:starter-kit` conjures arrives usable.\n\nA real arrival does not. `RimWorld/ScenPart_PlayerPawnsArriveMethod.DoDropPods`\nends in `DropPodUtility.DropThingGroupsNear(..., forbid: true, ...)`, and the\ntutorial agrees about how much this matters: `Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Instructions.xml`\nlists `UnforbidStartingResources` THIRD, immediately after the stockpile steps.\nThis is the standing path, not a drop-pod special case.\n\nSo today M1 (`664e9b9`) provably cannot rehearse `unforbid` against a real\nobstacle. That matters because it is the ONE thing on the M1 path that would\nexercise it, `unforbid` is already a shipped verb, and **the failure mode has\nalready been observed live**: comment #5 on `96d9315` records Evan finding a\nforbidden rifle, revolver, knife and flak set sitting unused while FSWA silently\nskipped them — `FSWA_MapComponent` checks `!thing.IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)`\nin three separate places. If the kit never starts forbidden, a ten-day run cannot\nsurface that class of bug at all.\n\nThe argument that decides it is not realism. It is DESIGN's own principle about\nfog: **an agent handed affordances no player has weakens the colony as a test\nsubstrate.**\n\n## Scope\n\nSmall. `dev:starter-kit` forbids what it spawns, matching `DoDropPods`'\n`forbid: true`. Deliberately NOT taken in session 10, which was audit-and-merge —\nadding untested behaviour mid-round is the pattern that round existed to stop.\n\nCheck whether `dev:spawn-thing` and the other staging verbs should follow, or\nwhether the kit is the only one that should: the kit models an ARRIVAL, while a\nbare spawn models a thing appearing, and those are different fictions. Decide it\nrather than making them silently inconsistent.\n\n## Investigate existing code\n\n- `Source/AutoRimmer/StarterKit.cs` — no forbidding anywhere today.\n- `RimWorld/ScenPart_PlayerPawnsArriveMethod.DoDropPods` — the `forbid: true` call.\n- `Verse/ThingExtensions` / `CompForbiddable` — the route to set it, and whether\n  setting it writes scribed state an observer would trip over.\n- The shipped `unforbid` verb, so the fixture and the remedy agree on rect shape.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `dev:starter-kit` leaves its spawned gear forbidden; a `things` read shows it\n  forbidden before any `unforbid`.\n- `unforbid` over the kit's rect clears it, and a pawn then picks the gear up —\n  proving the obstacle was real and the remedy works, not just that a flag flipped.\n- Journaled as a dev action like the rest of staging, so M1's no-dev-verbs-after-staging\n  invariant still reads cleanly.\n- Stated explicitly whether the other staging verbs follow suit or deliberately\n  do not.","files":null}]}